                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "983572b2-75ea-3687-a48d-d6d8754d4a8c",
                        "entry_id": "53b6d5cb-48f2-3ab5-90dc-a4f21534ffbc",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Suite de syllabes incohérentes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2ab64dee-c021-48bd-9880-ca0a5b76e799",
                                "sense_id": "983572b2-75ea-3687-a48d-d6d8754d4a8c",
                                "text": "Technique qui consiste à mettre des sons ou des syllabes les unes derrière les autres, le « gromelot » n’est compréhensible que par l’intonation et la gestuelle qui l’accompagne."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "eb110cfa-a965-4006-8be6-a380d98e4032",
                                "sense_id": "983572b2-75ea-3687-a48d-d6d8754d4a8c",
                                "text": "C'est le mot qui désigne, au théâtre, un faux dialogue, indistinct, destiné à n'être pas compris du public. Les grommelots sont produits sciemment par des acteurs afin de «meubler» une scène en faisant semblant de dire quelque chose, sur une intonation quelconque alors qu'ils prononcent une suite de syllabes sans aucun sens. Touf do boss ti mota mou, lancé d'un air étonné, ou contrit, ou bien d'un ton joyeux, ou encore sans aucun ton du tout, constitue à proprement parler un grommelot."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f6b236f8-61ea-47ca-bd5a-9db05f51bda9",
                        "source_entry_id": "53b6d5cb-48f2-3ab5-90dc-a4f21534ffbc",
                        "source_sense_id": "983572b2-75ea-3687-a48d-d6d8754d4a8c",
                        "target_lang_word": "grammelot",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "grammelot",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0ba8f35f-8a77-3db0-b16b-d31f352d9e32",
                                        "entry_id": "98061695-6eda-3db1-accd-b060fb1caa5e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A style of language used in satirical theater: a gibberish with macaronic and onomatopoeic elements, used in association with mime and mimicry."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "126c3916-d1c5-47ed-a86b-d2926612baa2",
                                                "sense_id": "0ba8f35f-8a77-3db0-b16b-d31f352d9e32",
                                                "text": "Actually, much of “Mistero Buffo” doesn't require translation and indeed is untranslatable: Mr. Fo speaks in “grammelot,” a kind of polyglot gabble speckled with an occasional intelligible word."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7798554f-a2e5-4f91-a6bd-5ee629b2a0d4",
                                                "sense_id": "0ba8f35f-8a77-3db0-b16b-d31f352d9e32",
                                                "text": "Then, dashing in and out of guises, using his expressive face and body and speaking in grammelot so that everyone can understand him, he might improvise. That would surely be a Nobel first, like the winner himself."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1f8e6681-4e7f-410e-97fa-ed7a99f9324c",
                                                "sense_id": "0ba8f35f-8a77-3db0-b16b-d31f352d9e32",
                                                "text": "The language sounds random – “moot moot” and the rest – but it’s actually quite precise. Theatrically, it’s like Grammelot, a technique that has been used in theatre and commedia dell’arte for hundreds of years."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
